1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,248
Command 3000 rpm, and this motor gets
there in under 10 ms,

2
00:00:05,288 --> 00:00:08,429
its current held at the 20 A limit.

3
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:14,503
Load the shaft with 0.4 N·m: the speed
dips 155 rpm and comes back,

4
00:00:14,543 --> 00:00:17,985
while i_q rises to carry the load.

5
00:00:18,025 --> 00:00:21,102
Two nested loops do this, a speed loop on
top of

6
00:00:21,142 --> 00:00:24,824
Lesson 19's current loop: field-oriented
control.

7
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:28,191
First, Lesson 19's answers.

8
00:00:28,231 --> 00:00:36,135
One: first order, with time constant 1/ωc:
159 µs at 1 kHz.

9
00:00:36,175 --> 00:00:43,543
Two: 1.5 samples, 75 µs, is 27° of phase
lag at 1 kHz.

10
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:48,266
Three: 5 to 10 times slower than the
current loop. Today we'll see why.

11
00:00:49,366 --> 00:00:53,721
Field orientation means steering the
stator current relative to the magnet's

12
00:00:53,761 --> 00:00:58,748
flux; on our motor, at right angles to it,
where each amp makes the most torque.

13
00:00:58,788 --> 00:01:02,903
The encoder gives the rotor angle, and
Park's transform turns the

14
00:01:02,943 --> 00:01:07,649
three phase currents into i_d, along the
magnet, and i_q, across it.

15
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:12,003
At 3000 rpm the phase currents alternate
at 200 Hz,

16
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:17,782
but in the rotor's frame they're constant:
i_d = 0, i_q = 10 A.

17
00:01:17,822 --> 00:01:22,595
The idea came from induction motors:
Hasse's 1969 dissertation,

18
00:01:22,635 --> 00:01:27,560
and Blaschke's 1972 Transvector control at
Siemens. With magnets,

19
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,454
the flux turns with the rotor: an angle
sensor is enough.

20
00:01:33,554 --> 00:01:37,123
Inside the drive sits Lesson 19's current
loop:

21
00:01:37,163 --> 00:01:43,896
every 50 µs it measures the currents and
sets the voltages, with a 1 kHz bandwidth.

22
00:01:43,936 --> 00:01:46,362
Around it, the speed loop compares the
measured

23
00:01:46,402 --> 00:01:49,176
speed with the command, and asks for a
torque.

24
00:01:49,216 --> 00:01:54,427
Next, a reference block turns that torque
into current commands:

25
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:59,504
for our surface-magnet motor, i_q* =
T*/Kt, and i_d* = 0.

26
00:01:59,544 --> 00:02:02,010
Each loop treats the one inside it as
ideal,

27
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:04,714
so the inner loop must be much faster.

28
00:02:05,814 --> 00:02:10,457
What does the speed loop control? Newton's
law for rotation:

29
00:02:10,497 --> 00:02:14,993
J dω/dt is the motor torque, minus load
and friction.

30
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:19,737
With the current loop doing its job,
torque is Kt times i_q*,

31
00:02:19,777 --> 00:02:24,958
so from i_q* to speed the plant is an
integrator: Kt over J s.

32
00:02:24,998 --> 00:02:33,643
So 20 A, or 0.78 N·m, accelerates our
rotor at 26,000 rad/s²:

33
00:02:33,683 --> 00:02:37,458
0 to 3000 rpm in 12 ms.

34
00:02:38,558 --> 00:02:42,522
Put a PI controller on that integrator.

35
00:02:42,562 --> 00:02:50,315
The proportional gain sets the crossover,
Kp Kt/J = ωs, so Kp = J ωs/Kt.

36
00:02:50,355 --> 00:02:55,088
The integral gain removes the steady error
a load would leave;

37
00:02:55,128 --> 00:02:58,875
put its zero four times lower: Ki = Kp
ωs/4.

38
00:02:58,915 --> 00:03:03,302
As the gain rises, the closed-loop poles
leave the origin on a

39
00:03:03,342 --> 00:03:08,129
circle around that zero, and meet at
−ωs/2: critically damped.

40
00:03:08,169 --> 00:03:13,260
For 100 Hz, ten times below the current
loop:

41
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:19,476
Kp = 0.483 A per rad/s, and Ki = 75.9 A
per radian.

42
00:03:19,516 --> 00:03:25,291
One price: the PI's zero alone makes a
speed step overshoot 13.5 %.

43
00:03:26,391 --> 00:03:31,300
Why 5 to 10 times slower? Because the
speed loop expects its torque at once.

44
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:34,087
Here's a small speed step at four
bandwidths,

45
00:03:34,127 --> 00:03:37,770
time scaled to each. Dashed: an instant
current loop.

46
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:43,703
At 100 Hz, the current loop lags just 6°
at the crossover:

47
00:03:43,743 --> 00:03:49,423
14.7 % overshoot, close to ideal. At
200 Hz, 16.2 %.

48
00:03:49,463 --> 00:03:56,887
At 500 Hz, 29 %. At 1 kHz, its 59° of lag
leave 17° of phase margin,

49
00:03:56,927 --> 00:04:00,940
and the step overshoots by 83 %.

50
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:08,317
Now a big step, 1000 to 3000 rpm. The PI
asks for far more than 20 A,

51
00:04:08,357 --> 00:04:15,583
so its command is clamped, and the speed
climbs at a steady 247 rpm per ms.

52
00:04:15,623 --> 00:04:20,453
If the integrator keeps integrating while
the command is clamped,

53
00:04:20,493 --> 00:04:25,424
it winds up to 2.6 N·m, and the speed
overshoots to 3950 rpm.

54
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:30,197
Anti-windup fixes it, as in Lesson 19.
Here, simply holding the

55
00:04:30,237 --> 00:04:35,748
integrator while the command is clamped
works best: 2.6 % overshoot.

56
00:04:35,788 --> 00:04:41,130
At 50 ms, a 0.4 N·m load: the speed dips
155 rpm,

57
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:49,578
and the integrator raises i_q to 10.4 A to
recover. i_d stays near zero.

58
00:04:50,678 --> 00:04:53,286
Why i_d* = 0? In the current plane,

59
00:04:53,326 --> 00:04:58,074
i_d across and i_q up, the 20 A current
limit is a circle.

60
00:04:58,114 --> 00:05:03,033
With surface magnets, Ld equals Lq, so
torque depends on i_q alone:

61
00:05:03,073 --> 00:05:06,394
lines of constant torque are horizontal.

62
00:05:06,434 --> 00:05:13,772
The circle's highest point is all 20 A on
q: 0.78 N·m. Put 10 A on d,

63
00:05:13,812 --> 00:05:20,727
and i_q drops to 17.3 A: 13 % less torque,
same copper loss.

64
00:05:21,827 --> 00:05:26,543
Buried magnets change that: the IPM
variant has Lq twice Ld,

65
00:05:26,583 --> 00:05:30,035
so negative i_d adds reluctance torque.

66
00:05:30,075 --> 00:05:37,944
The torque lines bend, and the best point
on the circle moves to 114.7°:

67
00:05:37,984 --> 00:05:45,924
i_d = -8.3 A, i_q = 18.2 A, 0.691 N·m,
15 % more than with i_q alone.

68
00:05:45,964 --> 00:05:51,160
Doing that at every torque traces the MTPA
curve, maximum torque per amp,

69
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:55,290
and the reference block reads i_d* and
i_q* from it.

70
00:05:55,330 --> 00:06:02,366
With MTPA in the cascade, the IPM reaches
3000 rpm 11 % sooner,

71
00:06:02,406 --> 00:06:08,185
and carries 0.4 N·m with 13 % less copper
loss.

72
00:06:09,285 --> 00:06:15,464
One more limit: voltage. With R neglected,
the steady-state voltage

73
00:06:15,504 --> 00:06:21,527
is ωe times the flux linkage, and linear
SVPWM allows 13.9 V.

74
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:24,440
In the current plane, that's an ellipse,

75
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:28,699
centred at −λm/Ld, and it shrinks as the
speed rises.

76
00:06:28,739 --> 00:06:34,700
For our surface-magnet motor it's a circle
around −130 A:

77
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:41,839
at 3000 rpm its edge is 91 A from the
origin; at 4500 rpm, 17 A.

78
00:06:41,879 --> 00:06:49,427
The IPM's ellipse is centred at −31 A. At
4810 rpm it reaches the 20 A MTPA point:

79
00:06:49,467 --> 00:06:54,964
that's its base speed, the top speed with
full torque.

80
00:06:56,089 --> 00:06:59,918
Your turn: a low-voltage FOC kit, the
motor with an encoder,

81
00:06:59,958 --> 00:07:03,027
and a brake or a second motor as the load.

82
00:07:03,067 --> 00:07:10,331
Use R, L, λm and J from Lesson 15, and
tune the loops for 1 kHz and 100 Hz.

83
00:07:10,371 --> 00:07:15,959
Step from 1000 to 2000 rpm, and log speed,
i_d and i_q.

84
00:07:15,999 --> 00:07:22,908
At the 20 A limit, the slope gives J: Kt
times 20 A, over dω/dt.

85
00:07:22,948 --> 00:07:28,158
Expect 2.6 ms at the limit, 5.2 %
overshoot,

86
00:07:28,198 --> 00:07:35,500
and with 0.2 N·m of load, a 77 rpm dip and
5.23 A of i_q.

87
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,880
Three predictions before next time.

88
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:43,794
One: the surface-magnet motor at its 20 A
limit. What's the best i_d?

89
00:07:44,794 --> 00:07:52,288
Two: the reference SPM at 24 V and 20 A, R
neglected. What's its base speed?

90
00:07:53,288 --> 00:07:56,436
Three: above base speed, what must i_d do?

91
00:07:57,436 --> 00:08:00,192
Next time, the series finale: field
weakening,

92
00:08:00,232 --> 00:08:03,406
sensorless control, and where the losses
go.
